# AWS Setup

## Step 1: Create AWS Account

Create an AWS account if you do not already have one.

AWS Console:

[https://aws.amazon.com/](https://aws.amazon.com/)

---

# Step 2: Create EC2 Key Pair

Go to:

```text
AWS Console
  → EC2
  → Key Pairs
  → Create Key Pair
```

Example:

```text
Name: ecommerce-key
Type: RSA
Format: .pem
```

Download the `.pem` file and store it safely.

Example:

```text
ecommerce-key.pem
```

Important:

* Never commit `.pem` files to GitHub
* Keep the file secure
* AWS does not allow re-downloading the key

---

# Step 3: Create IAM User for GitHub Actions

Go to:

```text
AWS Console
  → IAM
  → Users
  → Create User
```

Example username:

```text
github-actions-user
```

Attach permissions:

```text
AdministratorAccess
```

Note:
This is acceptable for learning projects.
Production environments should use least-privilege IAM policies.

---

# Step 4: Generate Access Keys

Inside the IAM user:

```text
Security Credentials
  → Create Access Key
```

Select:

```text
CLI / Programmatic Access
```

AWS will generate:

```text
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
```

Store these safely.

---

# Terraform Configuration

## Example EC2 Instance

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "ecommerce_server" {
  ami           = "ami-0f58b397bc5c1f2e8"
  instance_type = "t2.medium"

  key_name = "ecommerce-key"

  security_groups = [aws_security_group.ecommerce_sg.name]

  user_data = <<-EOF
              #!/bin/bash
              apt update -y
              apt install docker.io docker-compose git -y
              systemctl start docker
              systemctl enable docker
              EOF

  tags = {
    Name = "ecommerce-server"
  }
}
```

---

# Security Group Configuration

## Example Security Group

```hcl
resource "aws_security_group" "ecommerce_sg" {
  name = "ecommerce-sg"

  ingress {
    from_port   = 22
    to_port     = 22
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  ingress {
    from_port   = 8080
    to_port     = 8080
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}
```

---

# What These Rules Mean

| Port       | Purpose                        |
| ---------- | ------------------------------ |
| 22         | SSH access                     |
| 8080       | Spring Boot application        |
| Egress All | Allow outbound internet access |

---

# Terraform Output

```hcl
output "public_ip" {
  value = aws_instance.ecommerce_server.public_ip
}
```

Purpose:

* Exposes the EC2 public IP
* Used later by GitHub Actions for SSH and deployment

---

# GitHub Repository Secrets

Go to:

```text
GitHub Repository
  → Settings
  → Secrets and variables
  → Actions
```

Add the following secrets:

| Secret                | Value                   |
| --------------------- | ----------------------- |
| AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID     | AWS IAM access key      |
| AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY | AWS IAM secret key      |
| EC2_SSH_KEY           | Contents of `.pem` file |

---

# GitHub Actions Workflow

Location:

```text
.github/workflows/deploy.yml
```

## What the Workflow Does

```text
Push to main
      ↓
GitHub Actions starts
      ↓
Terraform creates EC2
      ↓
EC2 installs Docker
      ↓
Project files copied to EC2
      ↓
Docker Compose deploys app
```

---

# Example Workflow

```yaml
name: Deploy E-Commerce App

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

jobs:

  terraform-and-deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    defaults:
      run:
        working-directory: terraform

    steps:

      - name: Checkout Repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Configure AWS Credentials
        uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
        with:
          aws-access-key-id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
          aws-secret-access-key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
          aws-region: ap-south-1

      - name: Setup Terraform
        uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3

      - name: Terraform Init
        run: terraform init

      - name: Terraform Apply
        run: terraform apply -auto-approve

      - name: Get EC2 Public IP
        run: |
          echo "EC2_IP=$(terraform output -raw public_ip)" >> $GITHUB_ENV

      - name: Wait for EC2
        run: sleep 60

      - name: Copy Project Files
        uses: appleboy/scp-action@master
        with:
          host: ${{ env.EC2_IP }}
          username: ubuntu
          key: ${{ secrets.EC2_SSH_KEY }}
          source: "."
          target: "/home/ubuntu/ecommerce-app"

      - name: Deploy Application
        uses: appleboy/ssh-action@master
        with:
          host: ${{ env.EC2_IP }}
          username: ubuntu
          key: ${{ secrets.EC2_SSH_KEY }}
          script: |
            cd /home/ubuntu/ecommerce-app

            sudo docker-compose down

            sudo docker-compose up --build -d
```

---

# Docker Deployment

## Start Containers

```bash
sudo docker-compose up --build -d
```

## Stop Containers

```bash
sudo docker-compose down
```

## Check Running Containers

```bash
docker ps
```

## View Logs

```bash
docker logs CONTAINER_ID
```

---

# Accessing the Application

After deployment:

```text
http://EC2_PUBLIC_IP:8080
```

Example:

```text
http://3.110.xx.xx:8080
```

---

# SSH Into EC2

```bash
ssh -i ecommerce-key.pem ubuntu@EC2_PUBLIC_IP
```

Example:

```bash
ssh -i ecommerce-key.pem ubuntu@3.110.xx.xx
```

---

# Common Issues

## Permission Denied SSH Error

Fix:

```bash
chmod 400 ecommerce-key.pem
```

---

## Docker Command Not Found

SSH into EC2 and verify Docker installation:

```bash
docker --version
```

---

## Terraform Authentication Error

Verify GitHub Secrets:

* AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
* AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY

---

## Port 8080 Not Accessible

Check:

* EC2 running
* Security Group allows port 8080
* Docker containers are running

---

# Security Improvements for Production

Current setup is optimized for learning.

Production improvements should include:

* Restrict SSH access to your IP only
* Use HTTPS with Load Balancer
* Store Terraform state remotely in S3
* Use IAM roles instead of long-lived access keys
* Use private subnets for databases
* Use Auto Scaling Groups
* Use ECS or EKS for orchestration

---

# CI/CD Flow Summary

```text
Developer pushes code
        ↓
GitHub Actions triggered
        ↓
Terraform provisions infrastructure
        ↓
EC2 boots and installs Docker
        ↓
Files copied via SCP
        ↓
SSH deployment executed
        ↓
Docker Compose starts services
        ↓
Application becomes live
```

